true,"is_bank":false,"flags":[{"label":"Business quality","value":76.3,"unit":"/100","status":"good","read":"High-quality business (76/100).","meaning":"Composite of profitability, return on capital, balance-sheet safety and cash conversion (the Fundamentals-tab Quality score).","why_useful":"A durable, high-return business compounds value; a low-quality one needs a bigger margin of safety.","why_it_can_fail":"A single composite can mask a weak component; open the Fundamentals tab for the breakdown."},{"label":"Cash conversion","value":1.28,"unit":"\u00d7","status":"good","read":"1.28\u00d7 of profit came in as operating cash (5-yr median) \u2014 well backed.","meaning":"Median of (cash from operations \u00f7 net profit) over the last 5 years.","why_useful":"Profit you can't collect as cash is fragile; >0.8\u00d7 means earnings are real cash, <0.6\u00d7 is an accrual red flag.","why_it_can_fail":"One-off working-capital swings or a capex year can distort a single ratio; the 5-yr median smooths it."},{"label":"Balance-sheet safety","value":0.2,"unit":"\u00d7 D/E","status":"good","read":"Conservatively financed (D/E 0.20\u00d7, interest cover 10.2\u00d7).","meaning":"Latest debt-to-equity and interest coverage (EBIT \u00f7 interest) from the balance sheet.","why_useful":"Low debt and high interest cover let a business survive a downturn; high debt amplifies trouble.","why_it_can_fail":"Off-balance-sheet leases/guarantees aren't captured; a cash-rich firm can show low D/E yet still be risky elsewhere."},{"label":"Earnings momentum","value":null,"unit":"% YoY","status":"na","read":null,"meaning":"Trailing-twelve-month net profit vs the year-ago TTM (and whether the YoY rate is rising = 'accelerating').","why_useful":"Improving, accelerating earnings tend to drive re-rating; falling profit warns of a deteriorating business.","why_it_can_fail":"TTM can be lumpy for cyclicals/one-offs; cross-check the multi-year trend on the Fundamentals tab."}]},"valuation":{"ok":true,"is_bank":false,"pe_now":44.5,"pe_percentile":70.0,"median_pe":42.9,"earnings_yield_pct":2.25,"pb":4.9,"ev_ebitda":28.2,"quality_score":76.0,"pat_cagr_5y_pct":12.1,"peg":3.69,"cheapness":{"status":"weak","read":"P/E at the 70-percentile of its 10-yr range \u2014 expensive vs its own history."},"expectations":"At 44.5\u00d7 earnings for ~12% 5-yr profit CAGR, the market is paying \u22483.69\u00d7 growth (PEG). >2 = a lot already priced in; <1 = cheap if growth holds. This PEG divides TTM P/E by TRAILING 5-year annual PAT CAGR (endpoint-to-endpoint) \u2014 a time-axis mismatch vs the textbook FORWARD-EPS PEG, and one abnormal base/terminal year distorts the CAGR. A rough trailing valuation-vs-growth gauge, not a precise forward PEG.","cyclical_caveat":null,"meaning":"P/E percentile = where today's price-to-earnings sits within the stock's own last-10-yr range (low = cheap vs its past). Paired with the Quality score and 5-yr profit growth so a cheap multiple is judged against business strength, not in isolation.","why_useful":"Cheapness only matters relative to quality and growth \u2014 a low P/E on a declining or peak-cyclical business is a trap, not a bargain.","why_it_can_fail":"P/E breaks for loss-makers / one-offs; for banks use P/B not EV/EBITDA; a multiple cheap vs history can stay cheap if the business has structurally de-rated."},"ownership":{"ok":true,"na":null,"holders":{"Promoter":{"periods":["Dec 2025","Mar 2026","Jun 2026"],"values":[77.13,77.13,77.12],"latest_pct":77.12,"chg_1y_pp":-0.01},"FII":{"periods":["Dec 2025","Mar 2026","Jun 2026"],"values":[4.7,4.37,3.88],"latest_pct":3.88,"chg_1y_pp":-0.82},"DII":{"periods":["Dec 2025","Mar 2026","Jun 2026"],"values":[13.7,13.78,14.46],"latest_pct":14.46,"chg_1y_pp":0.76},"Government":{"periods":["Dec 2025","Mar 2026","Jun 2026"],"values":[0.0,0.0,0.11],"latest_pct":0.11,"chg_1y_pp":0.11},"Public":{"periods":["Dec 2025","Mar 2026","Jun 2026"],"values":[4.45,4.71,4.44],"latest_pct":4.44,"chg_1y_pp":-0.01}},"pledge"